{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W,$ respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:V\\to W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x\\in V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549937 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "15721919-affc-527a-806f-d54311da77ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549937 #edb/ref 1074 "The coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549946 #edb/ref 1069 "q-152903" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549946 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549946 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[9X+4].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x}{2},\\,0<x<2\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549946 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2595de8f-014d-5db6-a5d3-e22363b7564e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549946 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we simplify using the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[9X+4]=9\\text{E}[X]+4\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the expected value using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate the expected value of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[9X+4] & =9\\text{E}[X]+4 \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+4 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550021 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550021 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42981" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550021 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550021 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+8y=-28 \\\\ 2x+3y=11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $x$ can be written as $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{|\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|}$, where ${D}_{x}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550021 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fc096f25-da43-5ef0-8dca-9310fea097c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550021 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+8y=-28 \\\\ 2x+3y=11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{x}$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\,-28 & 8 \\\\ \\,\\,11 & 3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550033 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186550106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550033 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "24101b53-04f5-5a42-abcd-bf23100ae66d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550033 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550040 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "37f56bed-06b2-5321-bd27-98c4d186cd0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550040 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186549946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550057 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186550033 #edb/ref 1759